<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nepherites I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 393 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Nefaarud I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nepherites I was the founder of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, ruling from about 399 to 393 BCE. A general from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> in the Delta, he overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyrtaeus">Amyrtaeus</a> of the Twenty-eighth Dynasty, who was probably executed. He kept Egypt independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, sent grain and ships' equipment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> during its war with Persia, and was buried at Mendes, where remains of his tomb have been excavated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nepherites-i.jpg" alt="Nepherites I" width="320" height="356" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mario Leonardo Iñiguez · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neferites_sphinx_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nepherites I, known in Egyptian as Nefaarud I, is believed to have been a general from the Delta city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a>. In the autumn of 399 BCE he rose against the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amyrtaeus">Amyrtaeus</a>, defeated him in open battle and executed him at Memphis. He then crowned himself at Memphis, possibly also at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sais">Sais</a>, and moved the capital from Sais to Mendes. His choice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a> and the Golden Horus name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a>, both of the 26th Dynasty, is thought to show that he wished to recall an earlier golden age.</p><p>Evidence of his building work has been found across the country: at Thmuis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buto">Buto</a>, Memphis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqqara">Saqqara</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-egypt">Lower Egypt</a>, a chapel at Akoris, and a statue worshipped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmim">Akhmim</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> he added a storeroom and a shrine for a sacred bark. A basalt sphinx bearing his name, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> by the 16th century and once adorned a fountain in the gardens of the Villa Borghese in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In foreign affairs Nepherites resumed Egyptian intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, in 396 BCE he supported the Spartan king Agesilaus in his war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a>, sending 500,000 measures of grain and material for 100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. The cargo reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> just after the Persians had retaken the island, and it was seized by the philo-Persian admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon of Athens</a>.</p><p>Nepherites died in the winter of 394/393 BCE after a reign that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manetho">Manetho</a> gives as six years; his highest securely attested date is his fourth regnal year. The Demotic Chronicle says only that his son was allowed to succeed him. This son is generally thought to have been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hakor">Hakor</a>, who ruled for a year before being overthrown by an apparently unrelated claimant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psammuthes">Psammuthes</a>, and retook the throne the following year. A tomb thought to be his was found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mendes">Mendes</a> in 1992–93 by a joint team from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> and the University of Washington, identified by a shabti bearing his name, though definitive proof has not been found. Fish-filled vessels at the site may point to an earlier temple of the goddess Hatmehyt.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nepherites I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 399 to 398 BCE Amyrtaeus was overthrown by <b>Nepherites I</b> of Mendes, founder of the Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt; an Aramaic papyrus suggests he was executed.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-eighth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 399 to 398 BCE <b>Nepherites I</b>, a general from Mendes in the central Delta, overthrew Amyrtaeus of the Twenty-eighth Dynasty of Egypt and founded a new line.</blockquote><a href="/state/twenty-ninth-dynasty-of-egypt">Twenty-ninth Dynasty of Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
